Day 71 - Decode String

Problem Statement:
Given an encoded string, return its decoded string.
The encoding rule is k[encoded_string], where the string inside the brackets is repeated exactly k times. The encoded string may contain nested patterns.

Code:
class Solution {
public:
    string decodeString(string s) {

        stack<int> nums;
        stack<string> strs;

        string curr = "";
        int num = 0;

        for(char ch : s) {

            // Build the repeat number
            if(isdigit(ch)) {
                num = num * 10 + (ch - '0');
            }

            // Entering a new nested level
            else if(ch == '[') {

                nums.push(num);
                strs.push(curr);

                num = 0;
                curr = "";
            }

            // Closing the current nested level
            else if(ch == ']') {

                int repeat = nums.top();
                nums.pop();

                string previous = strs.top();
                strs.pop();

                string temp = "";

                for(int i = 0; i < repeat; i++) {
                    temp += curr;
                }

                curr = previous + temp;
            }

            // Normal character
            else {
                curr += ch;
            }
        }

        return curr;
    }
};






Time Complexity:
O(n) approximately, where n represents the size of the input/output processing.

More precisely, the work depends on the size of the decoded string because repeated strings have to be constructed.

Space Complexity:
O(n)

The two stacks store repeat numbers and previous strings for nested brackets, and curr stores the decoded result.

Approach:
We use two stacks:
nums → stores the repeat numbers.
strs → stores the string that existed before entering the current [.
curr → stores the string currently being constructed.
num → builds a possibly multi-digit repeat number.


